{
  "result": "Morning begins long before light reaches the windows, creeping across rooftops like a quiet rumor. The city stirs in small increments: a kettle\u2019s sigh, the elevator\u2019s distant chime, a newspaper sliding against a doormat. There is an unclaimed hush between these sounds, inviting reflection the way an empty bench invites a passer-by to pause. I like to stand at the window holding a mug of coffee still too hot to drink, letting steam blur the glass until buildings melt into soft watercolor shapes. In that moment the day feels optional, as if time were waiting politely for me to decide whether to participate. The first sip settles the debate. Caffeine sharpens edges, outlines tasks, resurrects urgency. Yet I try to stash away a fragment of that earlier softness, tucking it somewhere between a to-do list and a calendar reminder. Later, when traffic snarls and inboxes overflow, I retrieve it, recalling steam, watercolor towers, and the delicious possibility that everything might have turned out differently had I ignored the kettle, the clock, the sun itself and simply kept listening to the faint rustle of a city half-awake, murmuring its secrets to anyone slow enough to hear. Mornings teach patience: every beginning is at heart an invitation to notice before acting, to feel before deciding, to breathe before speaking. If I remember that, even noon\u2019s glare becomes less a command than a question, and dusk, whenever it arrives, answers gently, folding light back into silence so tomorrow can begin whispering all over.",
